cker compose up -d` recreate compose-mcp-1 on 8091 every boot","Plan: host port alternates `MCP_PORT_A=8091`↔`MCP_PORT_B=8092`, so after an even number of flips the live container sits on 8091 — the same host port compose-mcp-1 binds","Docker refuses two containers publishing the same 127.0.0.1:8091 host port; the second to start errors, and the sweep runs only AFTER both are up"],"finding":"The reboot recovery story has a host-port collision when the live blue-green backend occupies MCP_PORT_A (8091): compose-up.sh recreates compose-mcp-1 pinned to 127.0.0.1:8091:8091 while the --restart-always live container also binds 8091, so one container fails to start before the sweep can reclaim the redundant idle one.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fb63b0bb88cbb0889","impact":0.85,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section 'Run mechanism + the original compose container (bootstrap)' — 'On reboot compose recreates compose-mcp-1 and Docker restarts the --restart always live container; the sweep reclaims the redundant idle one.'","norm_id":"n0ec2a44d0cd33afa","priority":0.85,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A deploy flips the live backend onto port A (8091); the box reboots; compose-mcp-1 and the live rebar-mcp container both try to bind 127.0.0.1:8091 and one fails to start, leaving /mcp potentially pointing at a dead backend until the next tick"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"medium","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"high","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add an acceptance oracle: with fake `nginx -t`/reload forced to fail, assert the include is restored byte-identical to the pre-flip backup, the new container is `docker rm -f`'d, backoff is recorded, and exit 1 (deployed-sha not advanced).","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T12","T5b","T6","T9","necessity","project.portability"],"criteria":["E5","T5b"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":0.05,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Step 5: 'If nginx -t/reload fails: RESTORE the backup, docker rm -f new, record backoff, exit 1.'","AC lists 'Health-check failure leaves the OLD include byte-identical' but no oracle for a fake nginx -t failing that asserts the include is restored byte-identical and the new container is removed."],"finding":"The plan's step 5 defines a distinct nginx -t / reload failure branch (RESTORE the backup, docker rm -f new, record backoff, exit 1) that mutates shared state (a temp write + mv over the include), but the Acceptance Criteria list an oracle only for the health-check failure branch, not for the nginx-validate/reload failure branch. This failure path — which involves restoring the include backup and removing the new container — is untested.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8ddefb46b16ca7a4","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 5; Acceptance Criteria (Tier oracles)","norm_id":"n5c7bf6528e900b69","priority":1.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Mirror the health-check-failure oracle for the nginx-reload-failure branch, stubbing `nginx -t` to return non-zero and asserting include restoration + new-container removal + exit 1.","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"uncertifiable_outcome","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify how `mcp_run_new` reproduces docker-compose's `${VAR:-default}` interpolation for the `mcp:` `environment:` entries (bake the resolved defaults or apply the `:-` fallbacks), since `docker run --env-file`/`-e` does not interpolate them, and add an oracle asserting the run container's resolved env matches the compose service's.","cohort":["T3"],"criteria":["T3"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.431,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: `docker run -d` reproducing the compose `mcp:` service with `--env-file \"$COMPOSE_DIR/.env\"`, the same `REBAR_MCP_*` + `REBAR_OPCERT_*` env … a comment marks these MIRRORING docker-compose.yml's mcp:","docker-compose.yml mcp: environment uses compose interpolation, e.g. REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS: \"${REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS:-rebar.solutions.navateam.com}\", REBAR_MCP_AUTH_RESOURCE_SERVER_URL: \"${...:-https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp}\", REBAR_OPCERT_ENV_ID: \"${...:-9f1c8e42-...}\"","docker-compose.yml comment: a non-loopback bind (0.0.0.0) REQUIRES both the allowlists AND the TLS ack, or build_server raises McpStartupError — so an unresolved ALLOWED_HOSTS default would fail the new container closed"],"finding":"The plan's `docker run` reproduction of the compose `mcp:` service does not account for docker-compose's `${VAR:-default}` environment interpolation, which `docker run --env-file`/`-e` does not perform; several `mcp:` env values (REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS, REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS, REBAR_MCP_AUTH_RESOURCE_SERVER_URL, REBAR_OPCERT_ENV_ID) rely on those defaults.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f98630315158c2b41","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section 'Run mechanism + the original compose container (bootstrap)' — `mcp_run_new` helper","norm_id":"n8be79a9f7fe98791","priority":0.469,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Deploy where the operator has not exported REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS/ORIGINS into the autodeploy environment and they are absent from .env: the run container starts with the allowlist unset and fails the ADR-0050 non-loopback gate (McpStartupError), so /health never returns 200 and every deploy trips `mcp-unhealthy` rollback."],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"Have `mcp_run_new` source the same defaults docker-compose would apply (e.g. resolve each ${VAR:-default} in the helper, or pass an explicit -e for each interpolated key) and add a test asserting the started container's env for REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS/ORIGINS/REBAR_MCP_AUTH_RESOURCE_SERVER_URL/REBAR_OPCERT_ENV_ID equals what `docker compose config` reports for the mcp service.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State how mcp_retire_sweep discovers each managed container's host port to query its /health in_flight (e.g. `docker inspect` the published port for each container in the managed set), not only the live port from the include.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["E2"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.1812,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'gated by a \\\"still serving?\\\" check on its /health in_flight gauge'","'The live port is read from the include; the new takes the other' — only the live port source is named","'run a lightweight mcp_retire_sweep ... reclaim idle old containers' — sweep must reach each managed container's /health but the port-lookup source is unstated"],"finding":"The retire sweep must query each managed OLD container's /health in_flight gauge, but the plan does not state how the sweep discovers each container's host port (the live port is read from the include; old/idle containers' ports are not tracked there).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7d0a2960da332d58","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 6 + 'Retire-when-idle re-trigger' section","norm_id":"nf8fab4695b86956c","priority":0.4188,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Sweep over a managed set with 2+ non-live containers needs to curl each one's /health to read in_flight but cannot determine each container's host port"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"Specify that the sweep derives each managed container's host port from `docker inspect --format '{{...}}' <name>` (the published 127.0.0.1:<port>:8091 mapping) rather than from the nginx include, which only records the currently-live port.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State the fail-safe for an unreadable/erroring in_flight gauge in the retire gate: an unreadable gauge is treated as BUSY (do not kill), preserving the NEVER-kill-in_flight>0 invariant, and add an acceptance oracle for a stubbed /health that errors or returns unparseable in_flight.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T12","T5b","T6","T9","necessity","project.portability"],"criteria":["T5b"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.5312,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Step 6: 'remove older ones ONLY when idle; NEVER kill in_flight>0' — the read of the in_flight gauge is itself a fallible external call (curl to the container /health) with no stated failure handling.","Contrast with the memory pre-check which explicitly states 'Unreadable fails OPEN'; the retire gate has no analogous fail-mode statement."],"finding":"The retire liveness gate reads the OLD container's /health in_flight gauge to decide whether to kill it, but the plan does not state the behavior when that in_flight read itself FAILS (curl error, non-200, unparseable body). The conservative invariant 'NEVER kill in_flight>0' implies a read failure should fail safe (treat as busy / do not kill), but this is not made explicit, leaving room for an implementation that treats an unreadable gauge as 'idle' and kills a live container.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc8b55d019dc844ec","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 6; Retire-when-idle re-trigger","norm_id":"n510d1ab0787b11ea","priority":0.4188,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"Add a sentence to step 6: 'if the OLD container's in_flight gauge is unreadable/errors, treat it as busy and do not retire (fail safe toward retaining), unlike the memory pre-check which fails open.'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Pin the flip temp file to a name/location that CANNOT match the `mcp-upstream*.conf` glob (e.g. a .tmp suffix or a path outside /etc/nginx/) and state it in the plan, so the mv-over-include stays truly atomic.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.435,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["infra/nginx/rebar.conf.template:55 includes `/etc/nginx/mcp-upstream*.conf` (a glob, not a single file)","The existing RSYNC_EXCLUDES already treat `mcp-static-tokens.json.*` specially, showing sibling temp names are a known trap in this tree","If the temp is e.g. /etc/nginx/mcp-upstream.new.conf it matches mcp-upstream*.conf and both files are included until the mv completes"],"finding":"The atomic nginx flip writes to a temp file then `mv`s it over /etc/nginx/mcp-upstream.conf, but the include is a GLOB (mcp-upstream*.conf); the plan does not constrain the temp file's name/location, so a temp that matches the glob would transiently expose two `server` lines (or fail nginx -t) mid-flip.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f0c74f59a32f4942f","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 5 — 'write `server 127.0.0.1:<newport>;` to a temp file, `mv` over `/etc/nginx/mcp-upstream.conf`, `nginx -t`, `nginx -s reload`'","norm_id":"ncd089fcde416d3c5","priority":0.165,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An implementer names the flip temp file with a .conf suffix inside /etc/nginx/, so between write and mv the upstream briefly has two server lines (round-robins to old+new) or nginx -t reads an inconsistent set"],"severity":"minor","suggested_f